      "name": null,
      "problem": "$\\frac{6}{3+10\\text{i}}=$",
      "worked_solution": "The denominator is $3+10\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $3-10\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $3-10\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{3+10\\text{i}} & =\\frac{6}{3+10\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{3-10\\text{i}}{3-10\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18-60\\text{i}}{9-30\\text{i}+30\\text{i}-{10}^{2}{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18-60\\text{i}}{9-100{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18-60\\text{i}}{9-100(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{18-60\\text{i}}{9+100} \\\\  & =\\frac{18-60\\text{i}}{109}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18-60\\text{i}}{109}=\\frac{18}{109}-\\frac{60}{109}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
