      "problem": "$\\frac{8\\text{i}}{-4+3\\text{i}}=$",
      "worked_solution": "The denominator is $-4+3\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $-4-3\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $-4-3\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8\\text{i}}{-4+3\\text{i}} & =\\frac{8\\text{i}}{-4+3\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{-4-3\\text{i}}{-4-3\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-32\\text{i}-24{\\text{i}}^{2}}{(-4)^{2}-{3}^{2}{\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-32\\text{i}-24(-1)}{(-4)^{2}-{3}^{2}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-32\\text{i}+24}{16+9} \\\\  & =\\frac{24-32\\text{i}}{25}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{24-32\\text{i}}{25}=\\frac{24}{25}-\\frac{32}{25}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
